onal  a third of our channel and a third of  our chief executives are not British  they're not necessarily European they  come from all around the world and I  think what people want to be sure is it  still be possible for Britain to attract  top talent from anywhere in the world  the commitment from the minister  responsible today was exactly that more  controlled immigration but certainly we  need to be a magnet for talent  innovation and success and you're not  seeing any hesitation on the part of  international executives about coming to  the UK not really I mean some of the  banks have been saying they're making  contingency plans but then nobody's  anticipating a mass exodus business will  always head fair bet but the opportunity  is far outweigh the threats we are in a  negotiation process and so to be  optimistic would be I think ignoring the  fact that this is going to be a long and  probably volatile process what I am  confident about is that people are  engaging on it and are really trying to  be pragmatic and look beyond politics to  come to have outcomes I think it's  really business as usual still the UK is  a huge economy it's an important economy  both for asset management and for  insurance as the rest of the financial  services sector we've got many many  customers that are global customers who  need coverage for their businesses here  so know it's continuing business as  usual in trade there is a very big  opportunity for the Britain to become  world's beacon of free trade trade  global trade is stalled for the last 10  years a share of GNP and largely because  all the trade deals have been around  manufacturing and the next challenge is  to do services in agriculture and there  is the potential for Britain to lead the  way in doing a services for agriculture  deal and we are obviously one of the  world's leading services economy I am  really optimistic about the future of  financial services in the you know  Kingdom for one particular reason the 